    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p21-p2-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If a cube with a cross-sectional area of $2.5\\text{ m}^2$ has a terminal velocity of $2.9\\text{ m/s}$, what would be the cross-sectional area of a sphere which reaches a terminal velocity of $5.1\\text{ m/s}$? Give your answer to 2 decimal places.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$1.81\\text{ m}^2$.",
          "worked": "For the cube:\n$$\\begin{align*}k&=T^2AC\\\\k&=2.9^2\\times2.5\\times1.05\\\\k&=22.07625\\end{align*}$$\nFor the sphere, using the same constant:\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=\\frac{k}{T^2C}\\\\A&=\\frac{22.07625}{5.1^2\\times0.47}\\\\A&\\approx1.81\\text{ m}^2\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 30
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-p2-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": {
          "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
          "version": 1,
          "blocks": [
            {
              "id": "p21-p2-density-context-paragraph",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "The constant of proportionality (",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "k",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ") for the model above assumes that the mass (",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "m",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "), acceleration due to gravity (",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "g",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "), and the density of the fluid (",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "\\rho",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ") through which the object is moving all remain constant. That is, ",
                  "marks": []
                },
                {
                  "type": "math",
                  "latex": "k=\\frac{2mg}{\\rho}",
                  "display": false
                },
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": ".",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "id": "p21-p2-b-task",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "fontSize": null,
              "align": "left",
              "spaceBefore": 0,
              "spaceAfter": 2,
              "lineHeight": 1.4,
              "indent": 0,
              "inlines": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "text": "What would you expect the terminal velocity of the cube in part (a) to be if the density of the fluid through which the objects are moving is reduced by 10%? Give your answer to 1 decimal place.",
                  "marks": []
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$3.1\\text{ m/s}$.",
          "worked": "For the unchanged cube, $m$, $g$, $A$ and $C$ remain constant, so $T^2$ varies inversely with $\\rho$. The new density is $0.9\\rho$.\n$$\\begin{align*}T_{\\text{new}}^2&=\\frac{2.9^2}{0.9}\\\\T_{\\text{new}}&=\\frac{2.9}{\\sqrt{0.9}}\\\\T_{\\text{new}}&\\approx3.1\\text{ m/s}\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      }
